<DocsPage id="skills-and-commands">

          <p>
            A skill is a packaged set of instructions for a recurring kind of work — a house style, a review checklist,
            a deployment runbook. A custom command is a prompt template you keep as a Markdown file.
          </p>

          <h2 id="skills">Skills</h2>
          <p>
            A skill is a folder with a Markdown file named <code>SKILL.md</code>, in capitals, since on Linux the case
            matters. Its frontmatter gives the skill's <code>name</code> and <code>description</code>, and its body
            holds the instructions. Vector reads no other file name, so a skill saved as <code>&lt;name&gt;.md</code>
            is not found, and a <code>SKILL.md</code> without a <code>name</code> is skipped. The name comes from the
            frontmatter, not the folder; give each skill its own, because when two share one Vector logs a warning and
            keeps only one. The agent sees each skill that has a description, by name and description, and loads one
            by name, along with a sample of the files beside it.
          </p>
          <p>Vector looks for <code>SKILL.md</code> files at any depth under these folders:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <code>skill/</code> or <code>skills/</code> in Vector's own config folder (beside the
              <code>AGENTS.md</code> that <a href="/docs/vector-agent#repository-rules">Repository rules</a>
              describes), in a <code>.vector</code> folder in your home folder, and in the repository's
              <code>.vector</code> folders, from the folder you work in up to the repository's root.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code>.claude/skills/</code> and <code>.agents/skills/</code>, in your home folder and in the repository,
              from the folder you work in up to its root. Setting <code>VECTOR_DISABLE_EXTERNAL_SKILLS</code> turns
              these off.
            </li>
            <li>
              Each folder listed in <code>skills.paths</code> in your settings. A path starting with <code>~/</code> is
              in your home folder, and a relative path starts from the folder you work in.
            </li>
            <li>The skills downloaded from each address in <code>skills.urls</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The repository's folders are read only once you trust the repository. A permission rule for
            <code>skill</code>, whose pattern is the skill's name, can keep a skill from an agent: a skill it denies is
            left out of that agent's list.
          </p>
          <p>
            An address in <code>skills.urls</code> serves an <code>index.json</code> that lists its skills and their
            files, and Vector downloads them into its cache, one folder for each address and skill. When the index
            gives a skill a <code>version</code>, a changed version is downloaded whole and replaces the old folder;
            without one, files already downloaded are not fetched again. A downloaded skill too loads only from its
            <code>SKILL.md</code>. Skills are a Vector mechanism, not an MCP server.
          </p>

          <h2 id="commands">Custom commands</h2>
          <p>
            Your own commands are Markdown files in a <code>command/</code> or <code>commands/</code> folder, in the repository or in your global config. Frontmatter carries the metadata, the body is the prompt template, and the file's path becomes the name — so <code>command/review/api.md</code> is available as <code>review/api</code>.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector run --command review/api "the new billing endpoints"</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
</DocsPage>
